A company needs a **licence from the Reserve Bank under Section 22** to **start or carry on banking business**; doing so without one is **prohibited**.

**Discretion:** the Reserve Bank may **grant or refuse** a licence and may attach **conditions**; courts interfere only for **extraneous or unreasonable grounds**. It may weigh **defects found in an inspection (Section 35)**. A refusal leaves the company free to carry on **other business**.

**Section 22(3): the Reserve Bank must be satisfied that**

- the company can **pay present and future depositors in full**;
- its affairs are **not detrimental to depositors**;
- the **general character of the proposed management** is not prejudicial to the public interest or depositors;
- it has **adequate capital structure and earning prospects**;
- the **public interest** will be served;
- the **banking facilities already available** and the **scope for expansion** mean the grant will not prejudice the banking system or **monetary stability and economic growth**;
- any **other condition** needed to protect the public interest or depositors is met.

It may also ask for **capital above the Section 11 minimum**.

